    "turn_idx": 2,
    "turn_text": "I remember being a 10-year-old and, you know, asking my parents for a subscription to Newsweek, and they kind of looked at me like I was crazy. But luckily, they ended up giving me that subscription, and, you know, I'd read that thing, and then on Saturday nights, go hang out at my grandparents' house after we'd all go to church together. My mom was one of eight, so it was a big, big family. And after church, we'd walked onto my grandparents and it would be my grandfather, who was a firefighter, actually a lieutenant firefighter in Dubuque, and it would be my, and he was a Democrat, and then it would be my one uncle who was a small business owner, also a Democrat, and there'd be my other uncle who was a Republican and a lawyer. And then there would be me, the 10-year-old, sitting around that table talking about what was going on in the world. And, you know, I learned so much during those conversations and two things that stuck with me and really just dictated how I decided to spend the rest of my time in high school and junior high and high school and beyond how to do with those lessons I learned. And one was that even though I was a young girl, I had every right to be seated at the table as the grown men. Second, even though we would disagree, and we would, quite a bit actually, we'd hug each other at the end of the night and say, you know, love you, I can't wait to see you next week. And I remember thinking to myself that that's how public policy should be, that's how these discussions should be had.",
