    "episode_id": 84080,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84080.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 6,
    "turn_text": "What happened in the state of Iowa and how it happened was so wrong and so cruel that, you know, I know AFSCME made wristbands that I wore actually for quite a quite a bit. And I still have it that say never quit. And it made me think of that, every day I looked at that and I still think of it, you know, I think of that day and what was going on in my state. And it was actually that moment, you know, people ask you or ask me, you know, when did you decide to run for Congress or when did you know this is what you were going to do? Because I had no intention of running at that point. I'd been in the State House. I was actually my third year in the State House, so I was just beginning my second term. And then that moment happened and I remember looking up into the gallery and seeing, I mean literally my friends and folks who were like family. Again, teachers, corrections officers, bus drivers, folks who were just working their tails off trying to make a good life for their family and they were watching their government make their life harder. And I thought to myself, this is not how we treat people in my state or in my country. And I was going to do whatever the hell I could to get it back. Well, happy to say I'm actually supporting a bill that's being introduced having to do with actually protecting collective bargaining rights for the public sector. We need to be doing this. We need to make sure if the states are going to drop the ball. that our federal level is stepping up here and doing what's right and so excited to be a part of that. Also excited to see the movement and obviously my support of a bill that deals with workplace safety specifically in the healthcare industry.",
